<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ma Xiguang</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/947">947</a>–950 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ma Xiguang was ruler of Chu, a Ten Kingdoms state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunan">Hunan</a>, from 947 to 950. A son of the founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-yin">Ma Yin</a>, he was chosen over his elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-xie">Ma Xi'e</a>, who rebelled with support from the Southern Tang. Ma Xi'e's forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a> at the end of 950 and Ma Xiguang was put to death, and the civil war left Chu open to Southern Tang conquest.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ma Xiguang, courtesy name Depi, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-yin">Ma Yin</a>'s 35th son and a full younger brother of Ma Xifan, both born to the concubine Lady Chen; his birth date is not known. Ma Yin had ordered that his sons succeed one another by age, and Ma Xifan, who came to the throne in 932, was especially fond of the careful and obedient Ma Xiguang and had him oversee headquarters affairs. When Ma Xifan died suddenly in 947 without naming an heir, Zhang Shaodi and Tuoba Heng urged the claim of the 30th son, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-xie">Ma Xi'e</a>, but Liu Yantao and his partisans announced that it was Ma Xifan's will that Ma Xiguang succeed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liu-zhiyuan">Liu Zhiyuan</a>, emperor of Later Han, made him Prince of Chu in 947. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-xie">Ma Xi'e</a> came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a> to mourn, Ma Xiguang had his soldiers disarmed and kept him at Bixiang Palace, but refused advice to kill him and let him return to Lang Prefecture with many gifts. In 948 he refused a marriage proposal from Liu Sheng of Southern Han, who then sent the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eunuch">eunuch</a> general Wu Huai'en to take He and Zhao Prefectures.</p><p>In 949 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-xie">Ma Xi'e</a> rose openly, building 700 ships. Ma Xiguang's general Wang Yun defeated him and took 300 of them, but Ma Xiguang recalled the army, telling Wang not to harm his brother. Ma Xi'e then won over the non-Han tribes of Chen and Xu and the Meishan Tribes, who killed several of Ma Xiguang's officers, and submitted as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> to Southern Tang, which sent the general He Jingzhu to his aid. Liu Yantao's attack on Lang ended in an ambush that crushed his army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ma-xie">Ma Xi'e</a> advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a>, Ma Xiguang gave his main army to Xu Keqiong, who was secretly negotiating with Ma Xi'e, and ignored the warnings of the loyal officer Peng Shigao. Snow halted the armies, and Ma Xiguang turned to sorcerers and Buddhist monks, wearing monk's robes himself. When the attack came, non-Han soldiers set fire to the east wall, Xu surrendered and the city fell. Ma Xi'e had several of Ma Xiguang's officials executed and their bodies eaten by the soldiers. Urged by Zhu Jinzhong that a state cannot have two lords, he ordered Ma Xiguang to commit suicide, and Peng Shigao buried him outside the Liuyang Gate.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ma Xiguang” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>During a civil war between <b>Ma Xiguang</b> and Ma Xi'e, Southern Tang invaded and in 951 took Changsha, ending the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/ma-chu">Ma Chu</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/changsha">Changsha</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/china">China</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=28.2,112.97&amp;year=948" aria-label="Changsha on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7509 1131 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ma Xiguang"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7959 1401h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7959 1401h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=28.2,112.97&amp;year=948">What was here in 948 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
